# Aggregator browser cron — installed to /etc/cron.d by deploy.yml so a redeploy
# always re-establishes it (nothing manual to lose).
#
# The worker runs real Chrome HEADED under a virtual display (Xvfb, via the image
# entrypoint) because the anti-bot edges drop headless Chrome — see
# docker-entrypoint.sh. Two staggered one-shot warms plus a curl-gated heal; they
# share an `flock` (/tmp/mm-aggregator-warm.lock) so that even if the Keeta pull
# overruns its 15-minute head start, the noon/talabat warm WAITS rather than
# starting a second headed-Chrome container alongside it — at most one Chrome
# runs at any instant, which is what keeps this within the VM's RAM. VM clock is
# UTC; TZ=Asia/Dubai makes the hours Dubai wall-clock, one timeline with the
# API's AGGREGATOR_RUN_HOUR_DXB=23 pass.
#
# ⚠ RAM: a headed Chrome under Xvfb needs materially more memory than the old
# headless pull. On the 1 GB e2-micro a single warm dipped free memory to
# ~110–145 MB (survived, no OOM), but running this regularly wants a larger VM
# (e2-small/2 GB comfortably, e2-medium/4 GB with headroom). Size the VM up before
# leaning on the anti-bot warms.
#
# 1) Keeta order pull (every 3 hours). Keeta is mtgsig-signed in-page, pulled here
#    and pushed back — it is the ONLY channel the API's httpx sweep cannot reach,
#    so its freshness is set here, not by the API's rolling sales refresh. Pulled
#    3-hourly rather than once at night for two reasons: to promote the day's Keeta
#    orders while the day is still moving, and — the load-bearing one — to capture
#    each order's customer name/phone/address BEFORE Keeta masks them to `***` a
#    few hours after the order. A later pull that finds them already masked cannot
#    undo this: the ingest's `_PREFER_UNMASKED` upsert keeps the real value a
#    fresh pull captured and refuses to overwrite it with a mask. Every 3h (not
#    hourly) keeps the headed-Chrome minutes modest and never lands on the 22:15
#    warm. Was a single 22:00 pull, which routinely saw a whole evening of orders
#    already redacted. flock -w 1200.
# 2) Anti-bot cookie warm (22:15) for the TLS-impersonation httpx channels —
#    Talabat (PerimeterX _px3/pxcts) and Noon (Akamai bm_sv/_abck). Their
#    load-bearing cookie DECAYS: the API's httpx sweep replays it but never
#    refreshes it, so it eventually gets flagged and the session 401s (this is
#    what killed Talabat, 2026-08). The warm reopens the hydrated session HEADED,
#    loads a probe page so the edge rotates the cookie, and pushes the refreshed
#    blob back — verified on the VM: a headed warm rotated Noon's Akamai cookie
#    and pushed it `live` where every headless attempt stalled. Runs from the VM's
#    Doha egress, the SAME egress the ingest replays from, so the cookie stays
#    bound to the right IP/ASN. Deliveroo/Careem self-heal in the API sweep
#    (email/password via prepare_session) and are not warmed here — Careem must
#    not appear in this for-loop. flock -w 1200.
#
# A dead anti-bot session (the API flagged it `needs_bootstrap`) is now RE-LOGGED-IN
# automatically: `warm-sessions` defaults to `--auto-relogin`, so when a warm finds
# the stored state no longer authenticates it drives the stored login — Deliveroo's
# email/password, Noon/Talabat's email + Graph-mailbox OTP — and pushes the fresh
# session, all HEADED under this same Xvfb. Only a login that still needs a human
# (a captcha/passkey, or no connected mailbox for the OTP) falls through to a manual
# `login --channel X` (also runnable on this VM). The API health log still surfaces
# a channel that stays dead. Do not add a second UTC cron — one Dubai timeline.
# 3) Trigger-based re-auth (every 2 min). The host curls GET /worker/needs-heal
#    with AGGREGATOR_SESSION_PUSH_TOKEN from /opt/melting-moments-cakes/.env (never
#    printed) and only `docker compose run --rm aggregator-worker heal-sessions`
#    when some channel's STATUS is not live. It deliberately does NOT trigger on the
#    reported token_expired/cookie_expired flags: those fire heal for channels it
#    cannot help — Talabat's PerimeterX cookie has a ~5-minute nominal TTL yet keeps
#    working via edge-rotation (so `cookie_expired` was true almost always, running
#    a headed heal every 2 minutes all day and hogging this very flock), Noon's
#    Akamai cookie is refreshed only by the 22:15 Xvfb warm (heal cannot warm it),
#    and Deliveroo/Careem refresh their own credentials inside the API httpx sweep.
#    A genuinely dead credential still heals: the sweep flags that session
#    `needs_bootstrap` (status != live) when it cannot use it, which trips this tick.
#    If all are live, it logs and exits without starting a container. heal-sessions
#    now DOES run under Xvfb (see docker-entrypoint.sh): its auto-relogin spawns headed
#    Chrome, which needs a display, so a 2-min heal can actually re-login an anti-bot
#    channel instead of only detecting it. Worker backoff files live in the container
#    volume, not the API: a channel that is needs_bootstrap but in backoff will still
#    start the worker — heal exits fast when the channel is in cooldown. It SHARES the warm
#    lock (/tmp/mm-aggregator-warm.lock), so it can never run a second headed
#    Chrome alongside the warm. `-w 100` < 120s so a tick that cannot get the lock
#    (a warm is running) is dropped rather than piling up behind the next one.
SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
TZ=Asia/Dubai
# Every `docker compose run` is wrapped in `timeout -k 30 <budget>`: a wedged headed
# Chrome (a stuck OTP wait, a PerimeterX hang) is SIGTERM'd — then SIGKILL'd 30s later
# — so it can never hold the shared lock indefinitely. Before this, a talabat auto-login
# that hung once held /tmp/mm-aggregator-warm.lock for ~8.5h and starved every heal tick,
# leaving all channels dead all day. The single shared lock is KEPT (one headed Chrome at
# a time is what fits the VM's RAM); the timeout, not a second lock, is what prevents a
# hang from starving the others.
0 */3 * * * root /usr/bin/flock -w 1200 /tmp/mm-aggregator-warm.lock -c 'cd /opt/melting-moments-cakes && /usr/bin/timeout -k 30 1500 /usr/bin/docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml run --rm aggregator-worker warm-sessions --channel keeta' 2>&1 | /usr/bin/logger -t aggregator-keeta-pull
15 22 * * * root /usr/bin/flock -w 1200 /tmp/mm-aggregator-warm.lock -c 'cd /opt/melting-moments-cakes && for ch in noon talabat; do /usr/bin/timeout -k 30 600 /usr/bin/docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml run --rm aggregator-worker warm-sessions --channel "$ch"; done' 2>&1 | /usr/bin/logger -t aggregator-antibot-warm
# ⚠ cron.d treats a bare `%` as a newline (rest becomes the command's stdin), which
# silently truncated the old `printf "%s"` gate at `printf "` — so this heal tick ran
# NOTHING for hours while looking scheduled (the "warming cron runs but auth still
# 401s" symptom). The response is now handed to python via an env var (HEAL_BODY), so
# there is no `%` on the line at all and nothing to escape.
*/2 * * * * root /usr/bin/flock -w 100 /tmp/mm-aggregator-warm.lock -c 'cd /opt/melting-moments-cakes && token=$(grep -E "^AGGREGATOR_SESSION_PUSH_TOKEN=" /opt/melting-moments-cakes/.env | head -n1 | cut -d= -f2- | tr -d "\r") && if body=$(curl -sS -H "Authorization: Bearer $token" https://api.meltingmomentscakes.com/api/v1/aggregators/worker/needs-heal) && HEAL_BODY="$body" python3 -c "import os,json,sys; d=json.loads(os.environ.get(\"HEAL_BODY\") or \"null\"); items=d if isinstance(d,list) else (next(iter(d.values()),[]) if isinstance(d,dict) else []); sys.exit(0 if any((i.get(\"status\") or \"\")!=\"live\" for i in items) else 1)"; then /usr/bin/timeout -k 30 480 /usr/bin/docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml run --rm aggregator-worker heal-sessions; else echo aggregator-reauth: all sessions live, skip; fi' 2>&1 | /usr/bin/logger -t aggregator-reauth
